### Mobile User Plane Architecture
- **Presentation:** [MUP Architecture](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dmm-mup-architecture-00)
- **Presenter:** Satoru Matsushima
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-dmm-mup-architecture`
- **Discussion:**
  - Satoru Matsushima presented updates in revisions -03 and -04, addressing comments from the Routing Area Directorate review.
  - The standardization scope was explicitly defined: the MUP architecture draft specifies semantic behavior and architecture, whereas wire encodings and namespace allocations are delegated to the BESS working group draft.
  - Terminology (e.g., FTEID, session information) and deployment scope (identifying single administrative domains as the typical target while remaining open to multi-AS setups) were clarified.
  - The authors requested progressing the document to WGLC as directorate comments have been resolved.

### Architecture Discussion on SRv6 Mobile User Plane
- **Presentation:** [Architecture Discussion on SRv6 Mobile User Plane](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dmm-architecture-discussion-on-srv6-mobile-user-plane-00)
- **Presenter:** Teppei Kamada
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-dmm-srv6mob-arch`
- **Discussion:**
  - Teppei Kamada outlined updates in revisions -04 and -05.
  - Revision -04 clarified the $O(N^2)$ vs $O(N)$ connectivity state comparison and emphasized that 3GPP session management remains intact while the MUP control plane translates session data into routing information.
  - Revision -05 refined wording to frame certain trade-offs as "design considerations" rather than categorical limitations and clarified deployment dependencies for handover and scalability.
  - Tianji Jiang asked how 3GPP session information is exposed to the MUP controller. Sri Gundavelli recommended adding explicit system assumptions to the draft to clarify this interaction.
  - Authors plan to gather further WG review to determine WGLC readiness.

### YANG Extension for Attachment Circuit as a Service with UDP Tunnel Support
- **Presentation:** [YANG Extension for Attachment Circuit as a Service with UDP Tunnel Support](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dmm-yang-extension-for-attachment-circuit-as-a-service-with-udp-tunnel-support-00)
- **Presenter:** Luis M. Contreras
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-dmm-udp-tunnel-acaas-extn`
- **Discussion:**
  - Luis M. Contreras reviewed changes addressing WGLC comments in revisions -02 and -03, including YANG module name and prefix alignment, figure and example updates, and updated references (such as RFC 9607).
  - The YANG module was validated with zero errors and zero warnings.
  - Satoru Matsushima reported completing the chair write-up and requesting the YANG Doctors review. He noted that the publication request can proceed in parallel with the YANG Doctors review.

### SRH Reduction for SRv6 End.M.GTP6.E Behavior
- **Presentation:** [SRH Reduction for SRv6 End.M.GTP6.E Behavior](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dmm-srh-reduction-for-srv6-endmgtp6e-behavior-00)
- **Presenter:** Yuya Kawakami
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-dmm-srv6-gtp6e-reduced`
- **Discussion:**
  - Yuya Kawakami detailed updates in revision -01, which clarified SID construction rules, extended prefix length support ($a \ge b + 40$), clarified data plane processing when SRH is absent, and confirmed SID bit-field ordering to prevent bit-shifting gNodeB IPv6 addresses.
  - The document is mature and ready for WGLC once a minor reference adjustment (moving the BGP MUP SAFI reference to informative) is completed.

### BGP MUP Architecture over 6G User Plane
- **Presentation:** [BGP MUP architecture over 6G UP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dmm-bgp-mup-architecture-over-6g-up-00)
- **Presenter:** Krishna
- **Discussion:**
  - Krishna presented a proposal to extend BGP MUP to support 6G user plane features, such as integrating AI service policy IDs and compute/inference targets into MUP Control Plane (MUP-C) decision-making.
  - Sri Gundavelli advised the authors to focus the proposal on concrete protocol extensions required within IETF rather than broad architectural frameworks.
  - Satoru Matsushima noted the CATS-MUP effort as relevant prior art for policy-based steering in MUP.
  - Ketan Talaulikar (Routing AD) advised that architectural concepts must first be defined and consensus achieved in DMM before bringing any BGP protocol extensions to the BESS working group.

